== Li n,1

#display-equation($"Li"_(1\,1)\(x\,y\)= integral_(\(0\,0\))^(\(x\,y\)) d v_2 d v_1 + d v_12 d w_(1\,2)$) Here $w_(1\,2) = u_1 + v_2 - v_1$, then #display-equation($"Li"_(n\,1)\(x\,y\) & = integral_(\(0\,0\))^(\(x\,y\))\(d v_2 d v_1 + d v_12 d w_(1\,2)\)\(d u_1\)^(n - 1) + d v_12 (sum_(k = 1)^(n - 1) d u_12^k d v_2 \( d u_1 \)^(n - 1 - k))$) since inductively #display-equation($"Li"_(n\,1)\(x\,y\) & = integral_(\(0\,0\))^(\(x\,y\)) "Li"_(n - 1\,1)\(x\,y\)d u_1 - "Li"_n\(x y\)d v_2\
 & = integral_(\(0\,0\))^(\(x\,y\)) [\( d v_2 d v_1 + d v_12 d w_(1\,2) \) \( d u_1 \)^(n - 2) + d v_12 (sum_(k = 1)^(n - 2) d u_12^k d v_2 \( d u_1 \)^(n - 2 - k))] d u_1\
 & + d v_12\(d u_12\)^(n - 1) d v_2$)

Suppose $p$ is the path from $\(0\,0\)$ to $\(x\,y\)$ that give the value of $"Li"_(n\,1)\(x\,y\)$, $q$ is a loop based at $\(x\,y\)$ that induces monodromy, we can take $p$ to $p\(epsilon.alt\)$ to start at $\(epsilon.alt\,epsilon.alt\)$ and then take limit $epsilon.alt arrow.r 0$, then $p q$ can be homotopied to some $q\(epsilon.alt\)p\(epsilon.alt\)$ \ To compute the monodromy around $x = 0$, take $q\(epsilon.alt\)$ to be the loop $\(x = epsilon.alt e^(i t)\,y = epsilon.alt\)$ #display-equation($lim_(epsilon.alt arrow.r 0) integral_(q\(epsilon.alt\)p\(epsilon.alt\)) - integral_(p\(epsilon.alt\)) & = 0$) To compute the monodromy around $y = 0$, take $q\(epsilon.alt\)$ to be the loop $\(x = epsilon.alt\,y = epsilon.alt e^(i t)\)$ #display-equation($lim_(epsilon.alt arrow.r 0) integral_(q\(epsilon.alt\)p\(epsilon.alt\)) - integral_(p\(epsilon.alt\)) & = integral_q d v_2 integral_p d v_1\(d u_1\)^(n - 1) = 0$) To compute the monodromy around $x = 1$, take $q\(epsilon.alt\)$ to be the composition of $\(x =\(1 - t\)epsilon.alt + t\(1 - epsilon.alt\)\,y = epsilon.alt\)$, $\(x = 1 - epsilon.alt e^(i t)\,y = epsilon.alt\)$ and $\(x =\(1 - t\)\(1 - epsilon.alt\)+ t epsilon.alt\,y = epsilon.alt\)$ #display-equation($lim_(epsilon.alt arrow.r 0) integral_(q\(epsilon.alt\)p\(epsilon.alt\)) - integral_(p\(epsilon.alt\)) & = 0$) To compute the monodromy around $y = 1$, take $q\(epsilon.alt\)$ to be the composition of $\(x = epsilon.alt\,y =\(1 - t\)epsilon.alt + t\(1 - epsilon.alt\)\)$, $\(x = epsilon.alt\,y = 1 - epsilon.alt e^(i t)\)$ and $\(x = epsilon.alt\,y =\(1 - t\)\(1 - epsilon.alt\)+ t epsilon.alt\)$ #display-equation($lim_(epsilon.alt arrow.r 0) integral_(q\(epsilon.alt\)p\(epsilon.alt\)) - integral_(p\(epsilon.alt\)) & = integral_q d v_2 integral_p d v_1\(d u_1\)^(n - 1) = - 2 pi i "Li"_n\(x\)$) To compute the monodromy around $x y = 1$, take $q$ to be the loop $\(x = x^0\,y upright(" such that ") integral_q d log\(1 - x y\)= 2 pi i\)$ First it's easy to show inductively #display-equation($integral_(\(x^0\,y^0\))^(\(x\,y\)) d v_2 d u_2^(n - 1) = "Li"_n\(y\)- sum_(k = 1)^n frac(\(log y - log y^0\)^(n - k), \(n - k\)!) "Li"_k\(y^0\)$) Thus #display-equation($lim_(epsilon.alt arrow.r 0) integral_(p q) - integral_p & = sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) integral_p d v_12\(d u_12\)^k integral_q\(d u_12\)^(n - 1 - k) d v_2 + integral_q d v_12 d u_12^(n - 1) d v_2\
 & =\(- 1\)^(n + 1) integral_(q^(- 1)) d v_2 d u_2^(n - 1) d v_12\
 & =\(- 1\)^(n + 1) integral_(q^(- 1)) frac("Li"_n\(y\)- sum_(k = 1)^n frac(\(log y - log y^0\)^(n - k), \(n - k\)!) "Li"_k\(y^0\), y - 1\/x^0) d y\
 & =\(- 1\)^n 2 pi i (- g_n \( x^0 \, y^0 \) - "Li"_n \( 1 \/ x^0 \))$) Where #display-equation($g_m\(x\,y\)= sum_(k = 1)^m\(- 1\)^(k + 1) frac(log^(m - k)\(x y\), \(m - k\)!) "Li"_k\(y\)$) The variation matrix is #display-equation($Lambda = mat(delim: "[", 1; "Li"_1\(y\), 1; "Li"_1\(x y\), 0, 1; "Li"_(1\,1)\(x\,y\), "Li"_1\(x\), g_1\(x\,y\)- "Li"_1\(x^(- 1)\), 1; "Li"_2\(x y\), 0, log x, 0, 1; "Li"_(2\,1)\(x\,y\), "Li"_2\(x\), g_2\(x\,y\)+ "Li"_2\(x^(- 1)\), log x, g_1\(x\,y\), 1; dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down; "Li"_n\(x y\), 0, frac(log^(n - 1)\(x y\), \(n - 1\)!), 0, frac(log^(n - 2)\(x y\), \(n - 2\)!), 0, dots.h.c, 1; "Li"_(n\,1)\(x\,y\), "Li"_n\(x\), g_n\(x\,y\)+\(- 1\)^n "Li"_n\(x^(- 1)\), frac(log^(n - 1) x, \(n - 1\)!), g_(n - 1)\(x\,y\), frac(log^(n - 2) x, \(n - 2\)!), dots.h.c, g_1\(x\,y\), 1) tau_(n\,1)\(2 pi i\)$) #display-equation($omega = mat(delim: "[", 0; - d v_2, 0; - d v_12, 0, 0; #none, - d v_2, - d w_1, 0; #none, , d u_12, 0, 0; #none, , , d u_1, - d v_2, 0; #none, , , , dots.down, dots.down, dots.down; #none, , , , , d u_12, 0, 0; #none, , , , , , d u_1, - d v_2, 0; #none)$)

#display-equation($d g_m\(x\,y\)= g_(m - 1)\(x\,y\)d u_1 - frac(log^(m - 1)\(x y\), \(m - 1\)!) d v_2$) Justifies the differential equation $d Lambda = omega Lambda$ \ Monodromy of $log^m x\/m !$ around $x = 0$ is #display-equation($sum_(k = 0)^(m - 1) frac(log^k x, k !) frac(\(2 pi i\)^(m - k), \(m - k\)!)$) Monodromy of $log^m\(x y\)\/m !$ around $x = 0$ is #display-equation($sum_(k = 0)^(m - 1) frac(log^k\(x y\), k !) frac(\(2 pi i\)^(m - k), \(m - k\)!)$) Monodromy of $"Li"_n\(x^(- 1)\)$ around $x = 0$ is $2 pi i log^(n - 1)\(x^(- 1)\)\/\(n - 1\)! =\(- 1\)^(n - 1) 2 pi i log^(n - 1)\(x\)\/\(n - 1\)!$ \ Monodromy of $g_n$ around $x = 0$ or $y = 0$ is #display-equation($sum_(k = 1)^m sum_(l = 1)^(m - k)\(- 1\)^(k + 1) "Li"_k\(y\)frac(log^(m - k - l)\(x y\), \(m - k - l\)!) frac(\(2 pi i\)^l, l !) & = sum_(l = 1)^m sum_(k = 1)^(m - l)\(- 1\)^(k + 1) "Li"_k\(y\)frac(log^(m - k - l)\(x y\), \(m - k - l\)!) frac(\(2 pi i\)^l, l !)\
 & = sum_(l = 1)^(m - 1) frac(\(2 pi i\), l !) g_(m - l)\(x\,y\)$) Monodromy of $g_n$ around $y = 1$ is #display-equation($2 pi i sum_(k = 1)^m\(- 1\)^k frac(log^(m - k)\(x y\), \(m - k\)!)$) The monodromy representation $rho$ is as follows \ For monodromy around $x = 0$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1; #none, 1; #none, , 1; #none, , - 1, 1; #none, , 1, 0, 1; #none, , 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , 0, 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , frac(1, 3 !), 0, 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.down, dots.down, dots.down, dots.down; #none, , frac(1, \(n - 1\)!), 0, frac(1, \(n - 2\)!), dots.h.c, 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , 0, frac(1, \(n - 1\)!), 0, dots.h.c, 0, 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1)$) For monodromy around $y = 0$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1; #none, 1; #none, , 1; #none, , 0, 1; #none, , 1, 0, 1; #none, , 0, 0, 0, 1; #none, , 1 / 2, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1; #none, , dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.down, dots.down, dots.down; #none, , frac(1, \(n - 1\)!), 0, frac(1, \(n - 2\)!), dots.h.c, 0, 1, 0, 1; #none, , , , , , , , , , 1)$) For monodromy around $x = 1$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1; #none, 1; #none, , 1, , , ; #none, - 1, 1, 1, , ; #none, , , , 1, ; #none, , , , , dots.down; #none, , , , , , 1)$) For monodromy around $y = 1$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1, , , , , , , ; - 1, 1, , , , , , ; #none, , 1, , , , , ; #none, , - 1, 1, , , , ; #none, , , dots.down, dots.down; #none, , , , , 1, ; #none, , , , , - 1, 1; #none)$) For monodromy around $x y = 1$ #display-equation($mat(delim: "[", 1; #none, 1; - 1, , 1; #none, , , 1; #none, , , , dots.down; #none, , , , , 1)$)
